From 2d9f16aa2cba9b6c652ea848f719cc00f1650018 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 14:15:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(update): stop a stale dashboard backend through its
 retirement fence, never mid background review [daveey]

hermes update's dashboard step (_kill_stale_dashboard_processes, restart_managed)
sent a plain SIGTERM. The serve's shutdown then finalized sessions and
tui_gateway.session_lifecycle cancelled any running background review mid
provider call. The drain window applies an update at the first idle gap, and
a review starts right after every turn, so one is often in flight then
(David-MBP 2026-10-28 06:37:41 stopped pid 91625 this way). 4101ef7442 made
the RetirementFence review-aware, but this kill path never consulted it.

Now, on the update path only, each stale backend whose port and session token
are known (spawn ledger / host rendezvous / argv; env token or rendezvous
token) is asked POST /api/health/retirement prepare. While GET
/api/health/idle reports background_review:<n> the update keeps asking: the
fence lets the review finish, asks it to stop between API calls past 300 s,
and stops counting it past 900 s, which is also the update's own deadline.
On a permit the update commits (admission closed, no new review starts) and
then SIGTERMs. Any other answer (idle, other busy, old backend, no token) is
stopped exactly as before. `hermes dashboard --stop` keeps its immediate stop.

Tests (real child backend with a real RetirementFence over HTTP):
- review mid-call: stopped only after the review completes (RED: cancelled mid-call)
- review past the wait bound: winds down at a call boundary, then stopped
- wedged review: update gives up at its deadline and stops as before
- controls: no review, and no reachable fence, are stopped as today
